  "conclusion": "The LLM-consensus label ceiling (spec-metrics-ceiling-calibration.md WORKSTREAM 2) CANNOT currently be established with locally-available ~7-9B instruction-tuned models. Two independent candidate second judges from two different model families (Meta/llama3.1:8b, Google/gemma2:9b) were tried against the SAME qwen3:8b first judge and BOTH failed the mandatory 90% calibration bar, with the SAME specific, reproducible failure mode: over-prediction of relatedness -- labelling unambiguous domain-disjoint FAR-tier control pairs as 'mid' instead of 'far' (llama3.1:8b missed 5/30 control items this way -- registan/platter, mazaa/duration, tokri/law, junoon/auntie, bhanji/trunk; gemma2:9b missed 4/30 -- dehat/yogurt, registan/platter, mazaa/duration, junoon/auntie -- registan/platter and mazaa/duration fail for BOTH models, suggesting these are genuinely harder control items, not model-specific noise). Near-tier control accuracy was 100% for all three models tested -- the failure is specific and asymmetric to the far/unrelated tier, not a general judge quality problem. qwen3:8b remains the one PASSING judge found so far (96.7% overall, 100% near, 93.3% far) and is documented here as the known-good first judge for any future attempt with a genuinely different third model family. Per GG's explicit stop instruction, no third replacement model was tried and the bar was not loosened or re-tested at a different temperature to result-shop a pass. This workstream is closed as a final, honest negative result -- not a blocker requiring further escalation."
